\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Danish crown needs steadying occasionally-c.bank.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Per Bech Thomsen  \par \uc2 712 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2005\par 16:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 COPENHAGEN, Jan 27 (Reuters) - Euro outsider \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  now and again in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to fulfil its mission of keeping the crown stable against the euro, its governor said in a rare interview on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "Typically, we \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to avoid abrupt movements in the exchange rate," said Bodil Nyboe Andersen, who steps down in October after more than 10 years in office.  \par \par \uc2 Along with Britain and neighbouring Sweden among the old members of the European Union, Denmark has opted not to join the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But it has pegged its crown in a 2.25 percent band around a 7.46038 central rate against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 "We don't operate with a narrower band, but we keep it tight, typically slightly above the central rate," she said.  \par \par \uc2 Since the launch of the euro in 1999, the crown has traded between 7.4230 and 7.4670 per euro, or 0.5 percent on the stronger and 0.1 percent of the weaker side of the parity.  \par \par \uc2 Andersen said the bank buys or sells \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - often rather small amounts - to smooth out big capital flows which often stem from cross-border takeovers, local institutional investors' purchase of foreign securities or repatriation of funds and foreign investors' trading in Danish bonds and shares.  \par \par \uc2 "We tend to stabilise the market...it is easier to take it in the run-up than to step in at a later stage. It's a signal to the market that we are there if something big happens."  \par \par \uc2 The Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  mirrors the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's interest rate policy but on rare occasions moves independently.  \par \par \uc2 "If a development goes on over a long period and involves substantial amounts we will resort to changing the interest rate, typically by five basis points," she said.  \par \par \uc2 The spread between the bank's leading two-week rate and the ECB's refi-rate has been 15 basis points since June 2003.  \par \par \uc2 "History shows that even a five basis points cut could change the situation and stop an inflow for instance."  \par \par \uc2 Andersen said the crown's credibility was built up over the past 20 years and had left \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets with a clear understanding of the Danish fixed exchange rate regime. The central rate has been unchanged since 1987, when it was fixed against the then German mark.  \par \par \uc2 Andersen noted that the crown's central rate was maintained through the general European Monetary System turmoil in the early 1990s and a more local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis 1998, gaining a lot of credibility.  \par \par \uc2 "In fact we don't need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  very often because private banks do a lot of the stabilisation themselves, while still making a profit from it. That's fine, that's how the market should work."  \par \par \uc2 ADVICE TO ERM-2 NEWCOMERS  \par \par \uc2 Denmark joined the Exchange Rate Mechanism 2 (ERM-2) in 1999, when the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was launched. In June last year Estonia, Lithuania and Slovenia joined the club on their way to full euro membership.  \par \par \uc2 Andersen said new ERM-2 members should ensure their monetary policy only has one goal - to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable.  \par \par \uc2 "You cannot take into consideration the domestic needs of the economy. You have to use interest rates only for stabilising the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," she said.  \par \par \uc2 Denmark's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has no inflation or money supply target but adjusts rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the sole purpose of keeping the crown stable.  \par \par \uc2 Danes rejected euro membership in a 2000 referendum, but Andersen is personally in favour of joining the euro.  \par \par \uc2 "When you already follow the rules of the game you could as well sit around the table," she said, adding that recent studies show that having the same \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  promotes trading.  \par \par \uc2 "It's interesting to look into this because there could be an effect that we so far have overlooked in the debate about the effects of being an euro outsider," she said.  \par \par \uc2 Euro membership is not expected to become an issue ahead of Denmark's Feb. 8 general election. Polls show a majority of Danes want to swap their crown for the euro but a new referendum on the issue is not expected for the foreseeable future.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050127e11r00eyz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
