\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-Policymakers calm as euro surge gathers pace.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Nick Antonovics  \par \uc2 732 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Januar 2004\par 15:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BERLIN, Jan 6 (Reuters) - Euro zone policy makers said on Tuesday they were watching the speed of the euro's surge against the dollar but were not overly concerned at its current level, while German exporters remained bullish about prospects.  \par \par \uc2 The mild verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  supported off-the-record comments by officials in recent days indicating the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exchange rate would only become a problem if it reached $1.30.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading close to life highs just above $1.28 hit earlier on Tuesday around 1400 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 "It's true that brutal \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements are bad for everyone but we have to refrain from any pessimism on this issue," French Budget Minister Alain Lambert told French radio, noting most French exports were within the euro zone.  \par \par \uc2 "For the moment, we're witnessing an evolution which still remains relatively reasonable," echoed Belgian Finance Minister Didier Reynders at a news conference in Brussels.  \par \par \uc2 "What is worrying are two things. Either too fast an evolution which we're not really witnessing today but could happen or an evolution which is in total contrast with the evolution of inflation and growth," Reynders added.  \par \par \uc2 The official insouciance contrasted with evidence the dollar's drop was beginning to cause strains in Europe.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers reported \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep its crown stable against the euro and Goldman Sachs cut its investment rating on European car makers, citing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength.  \par \par \uc2 "The euro has risen to a level that has severe implications for U.S. and UK earnings, and increases the competitive threat from Japan," Goldman Sachs analyst Keith Hayes said in a note cutting Germany's Volkswagen (VOWG.DE) to "underperform" and BMW (BMWG.DE) to "in-line".  \par \par \uc2 A rise in the euro's exchange rate makes it harder for European firms to win export orders and reduces the euro value of profits earned abroad. It also makes imports cheaper.  \par \par \uc2 A monthly index published by Germany's HWWA economic research institute showed the cost of euro zone commodity imports fell 5.0 percent year on year in euro terms in December despite a 14.6 percent rise in their dollar value.  \par \par \uc2 ECB RATES ON HOLD?  \par \par \uc2 Financial markets pushed back expectations of when the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which meets on Thursday, may hike interest rates from a record low of 2.0 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Euribor futures for March, a gauge of short-term euro zone interest rate expectations, rose to a three-month high at 97.920, implying three-month money market rates of 2.08 percent, and bond yields eased.  \par \par \uc2 "The move in the euro is raising expectations about steady rates," said HBOS economist Richard Batley.  \par \par \uc2 "Growth could be highly impacted by the strength of the euro and there is an idea that growth is highly geared towards the export sector at the moment."  \par \par \uc2 Strong exports helped pull Germany, the region's largest economy, out of recession in the third quarter. Data last week showed a two percent rise in exports helped the country to a record trade surplus of 135 billion euros in 2003.  \par \par \uc2 German exporters maintained on Tuesday an upbeat outlook despite a warning from a leading economic institute that the euro's rise was making firms less competitive on world markets.  \par \par \uc2 Anton Boerner, the head of Germany's BGA foreign and wholesale trade association, told Reuters he was optimistic exports could grow 4.5 percent this year despite the euro.  \par \par \uc2 "That (forecast) remains valid up until now," Boerner said.  \par \par \uc2 Boerner said he expected the euro to average between $1.250 and $1.275 this year, clearly above 2003 levels.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't see us reaching $1.40," Boerner said, adding however the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could range as high as $1.30 or just over within the next three months.  \par \par \uc2 The DIW economic think tank said if the euro reached $1.30 it would be a problem for Germany's economy and at $1.35 the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should cut rates.  \par \par \uc2 "Monetary policy makers must take responsibility for the recovery. They can't just keep their hands in their pockets and say it will come," DIW chief economist Gustav Adolf Horn said.  \par \par \uc2 However, a Reuters poll of 58 economists found 51 believed the next move in ECB rates would still be up, while seven expected a cut.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040106e016003ck\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
