\par\par \b \uc2  Danish  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  raises key interest rates. \b0\par\par\uc2 163 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2000\par 09:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  COPENHAGEN, Sept 29 (Reuters) - Denmark`s \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised key interest rates by 50 basis points to 5.60 percent on Friday, reiterating its determination to defend the crown's fixed exchange rate against the euro after Thursday's bigger than expected referendum rejection of euro membership. \par \par \uc2  Danes voted 53.1 to 46.9 percent against joining the euro in a referendum on Thursday and the bank said there had been "certain pressure" on the crown recently, forcing it to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fixed exchange rate policy will be continued after yesterday's referendum," the bank said in a statement. "To avoid uncertainty about the crown's exchange rate, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's lending and certificates of deposit rates are being raised by 0.50 percentage points to 5.60 percent with effect from September 29," it said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's discount and key deposit rates remained at 4.50 percent. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9t062on\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
