\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Slips Against Euro Despite Denmark's Vote, but Climbs Against Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 617 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2000\par 14:35\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  A WSJ.com News Roundup \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar declined against the euro Friday, as traders seemed unwilling to test central bankers' resolve to support the euro despite Denmark's vote against joining the 11-nation monetary union. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also weakened against the pound, but climbed to a one-month high against the yen following weaker-than-expected consumer-spending data out of Japan. \par \par \uc2  Late Friday, the euro was quoted at 88.30 U.S. cents, up from 87.77 U.S. cents late Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The dollar traded at 108.15 yen, up from 107.65 yen late Thursday. The pound bought $1.4750, up from $1.4623 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The euro's gains came in the aftermath of the Danish voters' rejection Thursday of joining the euro. The vote had been largely perceived in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as a litmus test for the euro. And while the outcome wasn't unexpected, it added to a lack of confidence in the euro, which at its all-time low of 84.38 cents hit earlier this month, had lost 28% of its value against the dollar since its launch in January 1999. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fallen even further against the yen. \par \par \uc2  However, on Friday the euro was anchored in a tight range around 88 U.S. cents overnight, and the move down to 87.50 U.S. cents just ahead of the New York open was attributed to flows related to reports that the U.K.'s Vodafone Group PLC was about to sell its Italian unit Infostrada SpA to Enel SpA and possibly France Telecom. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders were hesitant to push the euro lower, fearing that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with or without help from other central banks, would move to support the euro if it fell too far, too fast. \par \par \uc2  Traders may test the euro's downside next week, said Lara Rhame, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Brown Brothers Harriman & Co. "The referendum's behind us now. The euro has lost any support on that front." \par \par \uc2  In light of Denmark's vote, the Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised one of its two key interest rates to reaffirm its commitment to a fixed-exchange rate policy for the krone. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its lending rate by a half percentage point to 5.6%, but it left its other key rate, the discount rate, unchanged at 4.5%. The krone has been pegged to the German mark since 1982, and when monetary union kicked off, the country linked the krone to the euro. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is permitted to vary only within a 2.25% range against the euro, though \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a rule \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the krone moves more than 0.5% against the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Separately, ratings agency Fitch IBCA Inc. affirmed its AA+ long-term foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating for Denmark, but it replaced its positive outlook with a stable outlook. \par \par \uc2  The biggest movement overnight was in dollar/yen, following Japanese data that showed that personal income and spending remained weak. The dollar triggered stops early in New York to hit an intraday high of 108.47, its highest level since August 22. \par \par \uc2  Spending by Japanese wage earners in August was 332,633 yen ($3,093) on average, down 2.9% in real terms from a year earlier, suggesting the recovery in the corporate sector has yet to filter through to households. \par \par \uc2  The market largely ignored U.S. data showing a modest rise of 0.4% in personal income in August, with spending up 0.6%. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2000 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020330dw9t01jan\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
