\par\par \b \uc2  Crown firms, bond yields ease as Danes vote on euro. \b0\par\par\uc2  By David Crossland \par \uc2 695 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2000\par 11:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  COPENHAGEN, Sept 28 (Reuters) - The Danish crown firmed and debt yields eased on Thursday as market players speculated that reaction would be limited even if the Danes vote against adopting the euro in Thursday's referendum. \par \par \uc2  The crown firmed slightly against the euro to 7.4611 at 0830 GMT from 7.4652 at Wednesday's close as opinion polls indicated a tight vote. Danish bond yields narrowed slightly, reducing their spread over European debt. \par \par \uc2  Economists and dealers attributed the moves not to confidence that Danes might vote 'yes', but to growing awareness that the market impact of a 'no' was likely to be limited. \par \par \uc2  "People aren't worried that much that a 'no' will cause that much \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  turmoil," said Keld Holm, head of research at BG Bank in Copenhagen. \par \par \uc2  Holm predicted that the spread of Danish 10-year bonds over euro zone bonds would widen to 50 basis points if the Danes reject the euro, and narrow to about 20 if they accept it. \par \par \uc2  At 0830 GMT, the benchmark 10-year yield spread over German Bunds was 35 basis points, down from 38 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Membership of the euro would tend to reduce the spread by lessening the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk of investing in Danish debt. The Danish crown has weakened slightly in recent weeks on fears that rejection could cause investors to pull funds out of \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But it has not tested the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  limits of the 2.25 percent band in which the crown is locked against the euro. And markets appear to believe the Danish government's pledge to keep the crown inside that corridor if the Danes vote 'no'. \par \par \uc2  Danish stocks were little moved on Thursday with the top-20 KFX index 0.3 percent down. Dealers expect little impact on Danish equities whatever the result. \par \par \uc2  Exit polls will give initial indications of the outcome from about 1200 GMT and the final result will be known at around 2030 GMT. Markets are expected to tread water until the afternoon when the first polls could cause volatility. \par \par \uc2  Much will depend on how clear the first exit polls are, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  MAASTRICHT TREATY \par \par \uc2  If the 'yes' and 'no' camps are as close as opinion polls suggest, the outcome may still be completely open by the time Danish markets close and any reaction will not be felt until Friday. \par \par \uc2  Denmark's referendum on the Maastricht Treaty on European integration in 1992 was such a cliffhanger that the running vote count was indicating a 'yes' just 15 minutes before the final result showed Danes had in fact voted 'no'. \par \par \uc2  One dealer predicted the crown would drift lower in the course of the day and predicted it would move in a 7.4600-50 per euro range ahead of clear indications of the result. \par \par \uc2  "Even if the referendum were to be defeated we view Danish bonds as attractive on a fundamental basis," Merrill Lynch fixed income analysts said in a market commentary this week. \par \par \uc2  "Danish fiscal performance is significantly better and macroeconomic performance over the last five years has been better than the euro land average," it said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  available to support the crown was "formidable," the report added. "Speculators are likely to conclude fairly quickly that testing the central banks' resolve is a no-win proposition." \par \par \uc2  The vote, so close that just 20,000 to 30,000 Danes could determine the outcome, could have a greater impact on the euro. \par \par \uc2  The Danish verdict on the euro could initially lead to sharp swings in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But financial markets expect the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop it up if a 'no' vote sparks heavy losses. \par \par \uc2  The euro was broadly supported on Thursday ahead of the referendum result and was quoted at $0.8840 euros at 0830 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters said the outcome could have an impact of 3.5 U.S. cents - a 2.0 cent fall if the Danes reject it, and a 1.5 cent gain if they say yes. But opinions on the impact are divided. Some see the euro shrugging off the result. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9s067af\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
