\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  options market braces for Danish poll fallout. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Deepthi Wickremasinghe \par \uc2 587 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2000\par 16:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 27 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options market is primed for sharp moves in the euro's exchange rate over the next few days amid concern that a looming Danish referendum could provide the trigger for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  If \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2  votes on Thursday against joining the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  the euro is expected to suffer a further denting of sentiment, while a 'yes' vote would be supportive. \par \par \uc2  There is also the risk that central banks from the Group of Seven nations could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  together to buy euros, as they did last week, and once again trigger a sharp spike up in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  There were even some fears that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could take place ahead of the referendum results, given that G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  took financial markets by surprise on Friday ahead of the G7 finance ministers meeting. \par \par \uc2  "The market is really pricing in a big move and is trying to buy options for Friday, to get a two-way bet in," said an options trader at a European bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Implied volatility, which reflects expectations of how much prices will gyrate, was at 18.0/19.5 percent for one-week euro/dollar options, according to Reuters data , up more than one percentage point from Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "Even though vols are high, the risk/reward means that it is still better to be long the front end, because of any gapping risk in spot," said the London-based options dealer. \par \par \uc2  "Being short options and trying to lead stop-loss orders is just not going to work because spot has the potential to move so much that you are not going to be able to stop yourself out rapidly enough." \par \par \uc2  DANISH VOTE SEEN A CLOSE CALL \par \par \uc2  The latest opinion polls show that voting on whether Denmark joins the euro is likely to be close on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  While the market has mostly been pricing in a 'no' vote over the last week, this week's survey of the latest polls showed a rebalancing of the public's view. \par \par \uc2  "Implied volatilities at the front end are firm and reasonably well bid following Friday's joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and with the possibility of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and event risk coming from tomorrow's Danish referendum," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Dresdner Kleinwort Benson in Frankfurt. \par \par \uc2  "Spot has been slowly creeping up towards $0.89 because some people are expecting that there could be some \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the referendum." \par \par \uc2  The euro/dollar spot rate was within half a cent of its day's highs above $0.8880 , less than two cents away from Friday's post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  peaks above $0.90 and up more than five percent from record lows set last week. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said if the Danes rejected the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro/dollar spot rate would trade lower and front-end vols would initially fall. \par \par \uc2  They said options buying in the middle of the curve would then come in, on the risk that the euro would fall to new lows. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said wariness of further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the euro could limit losses in the euro/dollar spot rate. \par \par \uc2  "A 'no' in Denmark is more or less priced into the market because euro sentiment has been negative for a while now," said an options trader at a bank in Europe. \par \par \uc2  "But if we saw \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  combined with a 'yes' in \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it would obviously have a considerable impact - more than if we had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a 'no'." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9r0643m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
