\par\par \b \uc2  CORRECTED - UPDATE 1-Danish euro 'no' lead dwindles. \b0\par\par\uc2 735 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2000\par 23:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      In COPENHAGEN story "UPDATE 1-Danish euro 'no' lead \par \par \uc2   dwindles in new polls please read in 5th para: "...from \par \par \uc2   7.4590..." instead of "...from 7.5690..." (correcting Danish \par \par \uc2  crown's close against euro in Copenhagen). \par \par \uc2  A corrected repetition follows. \par \par \uc2  By Peter Starck \par \par \uc2  COPENHAGEN, Sept 22 (Reuters) - Euro opponents in Denmark were in retreat but clung to their lead over supporters in two fresh opinion polls Friday, six days before the Danes vote in a referendum on whether or not to adopt the EU single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The latest survey by Gallup, whose daily polls have registered a successively tighter lead for the anti-euro 'no' side since September 19, saw opponents score 47 percent, down from 48 percent Thursday, while the pro-euro 'yes' camp advanced one point to 41 percent. \par \par \uc2  A new Vilstrup survey found 45 percent saying 'no', while 41 percent said 'yes' - a sharp shift from Thursday's poll, which saw euro rejectionists 10 points ahead by 48-38 percent. Vilstrup had seen the 'no' side's lead growing daily since Monday. \par \par \uc2  The statistical error margin is some three percentage points in both polls. Since the two research institutes began releasing daily polls in early September, trend shifts recorded by Gallup have been echoed by Vilstrup with a time lag of about three days. \par \par \uc2  The Danish crown, which is pegged to the euro in a plus/minus 2.25 percent fluctuation band, strengthened to 7.4582 in late U.S. trading from 7.4590 at the 1500 GMT Copenhagen close. \par \par \uc2  During the European trading day, the crown firmed for the second consecutive session, breaking back onto the stronger side of its central parity for the first time since September 12. \par \par \uc2  The euro hurtled up more than five percent against the dollar early Friday as central banks from Europe, the United States and Japan united in a concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy euros, pushing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  temporarily above 90 U.S. cents. \par \par \uc2  But the euro pared much of those gains, falling below 88 cents in early U.S. afternoon trade in the absence of further \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying. \par \par \uc2  A snap Reuters poll of economists in North America and Europe forecast the euro would be at 89 cents a month from now. \par \par \uc2  The beleaguered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has shed more than 25 percent of its value against the dollar over the past 20 months, falling to a record low near 84.40 cents Wednesday after being launched with fanfare at $1.17 in Jan. 1999. \par \par \uc2  EURO FALL HURT DANISH 'YES' \par \par \uc2  The euro's steep fall was mentioned by half of Danish voters in a recent poll as a key factor influencing their views in the run-up to the referendum. A widening lead for the 'no' side coincided with the latest bout of euro weakness that began in September. \par \par \uc2  Leading 'yes and 'no' campaigners in \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Friday any euro rebound sparked by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would hardly swing opinions again. \par \par \uc2  "I doubt ... that the exchange rate of the euro will have direct effect on the outcome of the election," Denmark's staunchly pro-euro Prime Minister Poul Nyrup Rasmussen told Reuters Television, referring to the September 28 plebiscite. \par \par \uc2  "We have passed that period where the euro's exchange rate plays a dominant role in people's mind." \par \par \uc2  Holger Nielsen, leader of the far-left Socialist People's Party and one of Denmark's most vociferous anti-euro campaigners, agreed. \par \par \uc2  "Danes know very well that the euro is a very weak and unstable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have no significance for voters when they go to the ballot box next Thursday," he told Reuters, adding: "The majority is going to vote 'no' to the euro." \par \par \uc2  Rasmussen dismissed the notion his campaign was a lost cause. \par \par \uc2  "Nothing is sure right now. It will be close," he told a news conference before the latest Gallup and Vilstrup polls had been published. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Mogens Lykketoft, who also thinks Denmark should join the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reasoning for the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - concern about the potential implications of recent moves in the euro - showed that the 'no' campaigners' attempts to frighten Danes to vote 'no' by portraying the euro as a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  were groundless. \par \par \uc2  "The big powers think that the euro is an undervalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that this hampers U.S. and Japanese exports," Lykketoft told Danish TV2 television news. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9m06sbu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
