\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW-Danish economy minister confident of euro "Yes". \b0\par\par\uc2  By Per Bech Thomsen \par \uc2 629 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2000\par 20:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  COPENHAGEN, Sept 20 (Reuters) - Denmark's Economy Minister said she was confident Danes would vote to adopt the euro in next week's referendum, despite a lead for the "No" campaign in recent opinion polls. \par \par \uc2  "I believe in a 'Yes' - that's what I am fighting for," Marianne Jelved told Reuters in an interview on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "It's clear that some of the polls show an unpleasant picture, but it's a picture that I cannot recognise when I meet people at rallies or in taxis," she said. \par \par \uc2  Recent opinion polls ahead of the September 28 referendum have given the anti-euro campaign a lead of up to 11 points. But the polls suggest that up to 25 percent of Denmark's four million voters have still not decided which way to vote. \par \par \uc2  It is among these wavering Danes that the strongly pro-euro minister said she saw hope. \par \par \uc2  "My experience is that when I get the opportunity to answer people's questions they change their minds. It's positive that information and dialogue works," Jelved said. \par \par \uc2  If Danes reject swapping their crown for the European Union's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  it will only have a limited affect on the Danish crown and bond yield spreads over the euro zone, she said. \par \par \uc2  "The financial markets have already discounted a 'No' although the crown could come under some pressure," she said. \par \par \uc2  "But this will be nothing that we cannot handle. We have great experience in tackling pressure against the crown." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Nationalbanken, was last seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the crown on Monday, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a record low against the euro of 7.4690. \par \par \uc2  DENMARK TO KEEP RATE POLICY IF "NO" VOTE WINS \par \par \uc2  Jelved stressed that in the event of a "No" vote, Denmark would stick to the stable exchange rate policy that has been the cornerstone of Danish monetary policy for almost two decades. \par \par \uc2  If the crown comes under heavy fire from foreign investors who hold some 350 billion crowns worth of Danish bonds, Denmark would defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within the crown's current peg to the euro and not widen the fluctuation band - as happened during the EMS-crisis in 1993. \par \par \uc2  "A fluctuation band of plus/minus 15 percent is totally uninteresting for Denmark. Otherwise we would not have fought for a 2.25 percent fluctuation band," she said. \par \par \uc2  Denmark was allotted a 2.25 percent fluctuation band around its central parity against the euro at 7.46038 in September 1998 by its partners in the so-called ERM-2 grid. \par \par \uc2  Although Jelved was confident that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could handle any pressure on the crown with the normal tools of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and higher interest rates, she said \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be more vulnerable if an outright majority of Danes voted "No". \par \par \uc2  "In the event of a new Asia crisis like we saw in the autumn of 1998, Denmark would be more vulnerable as we can no longer take shelter from the market perception that Denmark sooner or later would join the euro," she explained. \par \par \uc2  "The fact that we say 'No' in a referendum to the euro makes us more vulnerable, she said, adding that any future referendum could take quite a long time. \par \par \uc2  Euro opponents in Denmark turned the tables on supporters earlier this month after the euro's latest bout of weakness set in. Analysts say the euro's collapse and the shift in voter sentiment in Denmark are feeding on each other. \par \par \uc2  Jelved declined to comment on the impact of the euro's slide on the Danish referendum. \par \par \uc2  "Don't ask me. It's not my \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," the minister responsible for the Danish crown \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9k077le\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
