\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Anti-euro Danes stay ahead in new polls. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Peter Starck \par \uc2 593 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2000\par 00:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  COPENHAGEN, Sept 19 (Reuters) - Danes averse to signing up for the European Union's euro single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stayed comfortably ahead of opponents in two opinion polls published on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  On September 28, Denmark will hold a referendum on whether to join the euro, which has lost almost 28 percent in value against the dollar since its launch in January 1999. \par \par \uc2  A Danish 'no' vote could deal a blow to the already groggy euro, which hit yet another record-low against the dollar on Tuesday, as well as dim the outlook for fellow outsiders Britain and Sweden signing up any time soon and possibly complicate so-called enlargement talks about opening the EU's doors for up to a dozen new member states, mainly from Eastern Europe. \par \par \uc2  The latest daily Gallup poll gave the anti-euro 'no' side 49 percent, unchanged from Monday, while the pro-euro 'yes' camp edged one point up to 38 percent, national DR1 television's text-TV news said. The rest, 13 percent, were undecided. \par \par \uc2  A daily Vilstrup survey gave opponents 45 percent compared with 42 percent for supporters, the daily newspaper Politiken said on its Internet Web site. Vilstrup also found 13 percent undecided. In the previous Vilstrup survey published on Monday opponents led supporters by 44-42 percent. \par \par \uc2  Both polls' margin of error is three percentage points. \par \par \uc2  Earlier on Tuesday, analysts said Denmark's europhile political establishment had begun preparing for defeat in the euro referendum, which Prime Minister Poul Nyrup Rasmussen has described as Denmark's most important foreign policy decision since Danes voted in 1972 to join the then European Community. \par \par \uc2  "The 'yes' side is growing increasingly desperate and people are already preparing to 'wash their hands' in case of defeat," Danske Bank, Denmark's biggest financial group, said in a daily report. \par \par \uc2  EU CRISIS \par \par \uc2  A Danish 'no' to the euro could heighten a sense of crisis in the European Union, already reeling from its battered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , widespread tax revolts and unease over a heavy-handed policy towards Austria, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  Popular support for the EU could sink to new lows, triggering a fresh sell-off of the euro, which retreated to new lifetime lows against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as supportive words from a host of top European officials proved no match for bearish market sentiment. \par \par \uc2  Europe's beleaguered single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sank to fresh record lows against the dollar in early U.S. trade, as words of concern about the euro from French Prime Minister Lionel Jospin and his Italian counterpart Guiliano Amato, failed to lend it much support. \par \par \uc2  "As soon as some guys out of Europe start speaking about the weak euro everyone just starts selling," said Jan Souleyman, senior yen trader at Credit Agricole Indosuez. \par \par \uc2  "Talk is cheap, if they want to get their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stronger then they are going to have to act," he added. \par \par \uc2  The euro slid a new low of 84.77 cents in U.S. trading, having slumped below 85 cents and below 91 yen to fresh records against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asian trade. \par \par \uc2  Euro opponents in Denmark turned the tables on supporters earlier this month after the euro's latest bout of weakness set in on September 6. Analysts say the euro's collapse and the shift in voter sentiment in \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2  are feeding on each other. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said only coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  between the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Bank of Japan and the U.S. Federal Reserve would have a lasting effect on the euro. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9j072va\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
