\par\par \b \uc2  Danish  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  market makers to widen EUR/DKK spread. \b0\par\par\uc2 313 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2000\par 17:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  COPENHAGEN, Sept 15 (Reuters) - Market makers in the Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market have decided to widen the bid and ask spread for interbank euro quotes against the Danish crown , chief dealer Tom Pietraszek at Unibank Markets said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The move is to secure an efficient market amid greater volatility ahead of the country's knife-edge September 28 referendum on whether to join the euro. \par \par \uc2  Market makers also agreed to widen the spread even further when trading resumes on September 29, the day after the vote, he told Reuters by telephone. \par \par \uc2  "We (market makers) have decided to widen the spread marginally with effect from (next) Monday," Pietraszek told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  "We want to retain a sensible and liquid market in order to service our clients," he explained. \par \par \uc2  As of Monday, September 18, market makers in Copenhagen will quote EUR/DKK with a spread of five points (i.e. 7.4670/75) for amounts up to 20 million euros, up from three points (i.e. 7.4670/73) under the current market-maker agreement, said another \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer, who asked not to be named. \par \par \uc2  "On the 29th we expect higher volatility than normally in the exchange rate," Pietraszek said. \par \par \uc2  "It is therefore likely that we start up with a somewhat wider spread. (It would be) widest in the event of a 'No'." \par \par \uc2  Earlier on Friday, \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Nationalbanken, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, buying crowns for euros, European dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Nationalbanken reportedly bought crowns at 7.4675 per euro, its lowest level since the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was launched on January 1, 1999. \par \par \uc2  Analysts attributed the crown's fall to record lows against the euro to Danish euro opponents' widening lead over supporters in the latest opinion polls. \par \par \uc2  With almost one-fifth of voters undecided, analysts say the outcome is too close to call. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9f07u0k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
