\par\par \b \uc2  POLL-Crown's euro peg to hold even if Danes say no to EMU. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Penny MacRae \par \uc2 677 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 2000\par 12:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 18 (Reuters) - The Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will stay hitched to the euro even if Danes vote "no" to joining European monetary union in a referendum next month, economists say. \par \par \uc2  According to the median forecast of 35 economists surveyed by Reuters, there is an 85 percent chance that the crown's peg to the euro will hold for at least 12 months if Danes reject the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the September 28 vote. \par \par \uc2  "The Danes love the link," said Jens Peter Neergard of Den Danske Bank in Copenhagen. For nearly two decades, the crown has been wedded to the German mark or euro even though Denmark opted out of the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's launch last year. \par \par \uc2  At the moment the referendum is too close to call with opinion polls showing the "yes" and "no" forces running neck and neck. \par \par \uc2  But there is strong support across the political spectrum for maintaining the euro peg in event of a "no" vote. \par \par \uc2  "No politician has advocated Denmark giving up its policy of exchange rate stability between the Danish crown and the euro," said Niels Christensen at Societe Generale in Paris. \par \par \uc2  The pledges of Danish authorities and, more significantly, the powerful European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the link even if Denmark stays out of EMU should deter speculators from trying to drive down the crown , economists said. \par \par \uc2  WHO WANTS TO TAKE ON ECB? \par \par \uc2  "Why would you want to bet against the ECB? I don't think there's an appetite to speculate against the crown," said Christel Aranda-Hassel at CSFB in London. \par \par \uc2  ECB president Wim Duisenberg pledged in July that the ECB "will do everything in its power" to keep Denmark in ERM-2, which is the waiting room for would-be EMU members." \par \par \uc2  At its request, Denmark has a narrow 2.25 percent deviation band around its central rate of 7.46 per euro, not the standard 15 percent band that applies to Greece, the other ERM-2 member. \par \par \uc2  Having made 'appropriate use' of its own foreign reserve holdings, countries can draw on a three-month financing facility at the ECB which should in principle be automatically available and unlimited, said Holger Schmieding of Merrill Lynch. \par \par \uc2  While the ECB is under no formal obligation to lend indefinite support, "the ECB is unlikely to let Denmark down", said Schmieding. "A Danish devaluation, a widening of the bands or a forced exit of Denmark from the ERM2 would be probably seen as a defeat for the ECB and a further blow to the bank's reputation - an outcome the ECB will probably want to avoid." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators made vast profits when they forced the British pound's exit from the original exchange rate mechanism in 1992. Already, the Danish crown has come under pressure because of some polls favouring a "no" vote and the National Bank has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  RAISE RATES? \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first line of defence against a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  attack might be to raise interest rates modestly, economists said. Speculators could possibly overcome any resistance by the Danish national bank whose reserves have been falling in recent months. \par \par \uc2  "But a clear sign the big ECB will let Denmark draw on an unlimited credit line...should drive the speculators away," Schmieding said. \par \par \uc2  Economists added Danish M2 money supply represents just 1.7 percent of the Euro zone total so ECB support for the crown would only have a negligible effect on Euroland money supply. \par \par \uc2  Still, not all economists are so certain the link would hold. "Under a "no" scenario, Denmark would be the country with the largest reservations toward the euro and a formal link to the euro," said Teis Knuthsen of SEB Merchant Banking. \par \par \uc2  "A \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis will occur at the point where the Danish economy warrants an easier economic policy and the euro zone is tightening. A fixed exchange rate system is not sustainable if there is no planned convergence," he said. \par \par \uc2  FOR DETAILS OF POLL, DOUBLE-CLICK ON . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw8i0aztt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
