\par\par \b \uc2  Little Relief Likely For Danish Krone Ahead Of Euro Vote \b0\par\par\uc2  By Poul Funder Larsen \par \uc2 699 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2000\par 14:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  COPENHAGEN -(Dow Jones)- These are turbulent days for the Danish krone, with the outcome of the referendum over Danish euro membership in the balance and some economists predicting sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation against the euro. \par \par \uc2  "Pressure on the Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will intensify" as the Sept. 28 referendum comes closer, predicted Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Bear Stearns in London. \par \par \uc2  "The Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be forced to accept a move up to DKK7.50 to the euro by the time of the referendum." \par \par \uc2  A rejection of the euro by Danish voters could send the euro as high as DKK7.60, according to Barrow. \par \par \uc2  However, some investors may wonder why there's all this fuss. Tuesday, the euro was trading around DKK7.4643, still firmly within the band set under the European exchange rate mechanism. Under the ERM II agreement, the Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can fluctuate by 2.25% on either side of a central parity of DKK7.46038 to the euro. \par \par \uc2  And since the beginning of the year, when the krone was stronger than its parity rate, it has depreciated by a modest 0.3%. Even if the krone were to weaken to DKK7.50 or DKK7.60 to the euro, it would still be inside the official band. \par \par \uc2  However, analysts say that for the usually rock-steady Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such a depreciation would be a big thing. \par \par \uc2  "The de facto band inside which the krone fluctuates is far narrower than the official one. Since the ERM II came into force, the fluctuations on either side of the parity rate have been below 0.5%," said Johnny Petersen, an economist with Jyske Bank, a Danish regional bank. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, growing doubts over the referendum's outcome threatens to widen the trading band within the trading band. A slew of recent opinion polls have shown gains for the "no" camp. An influential monthly poll, released last week by Danish business daily newspaper Boersen, puts the "no" side ahead of the "yes" vote by 50% to 43%. \par \par \uc2  That has intensified pressure on the krone, a process reflected in money market rates. \par \par \uc2  "We are seeing quite significant pressure building up in the forward rate agreements," Barrow of Bear Stearns noted. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Krone Likely To Stay Within 2.25% Band\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In spite of the increased volatility and uncertainty, most economists\par expect the 2.25% band to remain intact, even if the krone is trading\par with wider swings within it.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "In recent weeks the likelihood of the krone remaining inside its\par official band has grown considerably," said Jyske Bank's Petersen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The krone found some support from European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Wim\par Duisenberg, who last week reiterated that the ECB remains committed to\par defending the Danish krone, even in the case of a no vote. Under the\par bilateral agreement between \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Denmark\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the ECB, the ECB is obliged to\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the krone if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were to move out of its\par band.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "What we might see is a sliding process, where the shock effect of a\par 'no' is gradually being priced in," Petersen said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The nervous krone situation is already taking its toll on the Danish\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's war chest.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Danish foreign reserves dropped DKK9.9 billion in June to a total\par DKK122.8 billion. During the first half of the year, reserves decreased\par DKK42.5 billion, or 26%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, not all of that was spent defending the krone. Up to half the\par decline is attributable to the government's repayment of foreign credits\par and investors shifting out of Danish assets into international\par technology stocks early in the year, economists say.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Nevertheless, some market players believe the Danish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may\par have to resort to rate hikes, if pressure on the krone continues in the\par run-up to the referendum.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The Danish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can find itself in a position where it has to\par press up interest rates quite aggressively," Barrow said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Denmark's benchmark lending rate currently stands at 4.70%, 45 basis\par points above the ECB refinancing rate.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Poul Funder Larsen, Dow Jones Newswires; +45 33111524;\par poul.larsen@dowjones.com\par -0- 11/07/00 12-56G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw7b03dl3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
