\par\par \b \uc2  Swedish crown seen at risk if Danes snub euro. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lisa Jucca \par \uc2 683 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juni 2000\par 14:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 16 (Reuters) - A Danish rejection of euro entry in a referendum this autumn would have a spillover effect on neighbouring Sweden to the point of hurting the Swedish crown, analysts said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Waning support among Danes for euro membership has already started to dent the Danish crown's value against the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , forcing \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on June 2. \par \par \uc2  Such scepticism could claim a second victim - the Swedish crown - if investors start thinking that a Danish 'No' to the euro could jeopardise Sweden's expected bid to join the euro. \par \par \uc2  "If Denmark gives a resounding 'No', it would make it very difficult for the Swedish government to sell the idea of joining the euro to the people and the Swedish crown could come under some speculative attack," said Danny Gabay, economist at JP Morgan in London. \par \par \uc2  "We could therefore see some selling of Swedish crowns." \par \par \uc2  The Swedish crown was trading around 8.25 against the euro on Friday, up about 14 percent since the euro's launch in January 1999. \par \par \uc2  "There will be some spillover effect (from the Danish vote)," said Steve Barrow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bear Stearns. He expects the Swedish crown to be trading around 8.40 in three months time. \par \par \uc2  EURO FOCUS TO HEAT UP NORDIC SUMMER \par \par \uc2  Financial markets in Sweden and Denmark, which conduct less than 10 percent of their trade with each other, are expected to become increasingly focused on the euro debate in the run-up to the Danish vote on September 28. \par \par \uc2  More than the economic ties, the psychological link could play a big role in influencing sentiment in the two Nordic countries given the public has so far snubbed their leaders' eagerness to join the euro in both countries. Only last week, Swedish Prime Minister Goran Persson said a rejection of the euro in Denmark would have a great impact on Swedish public opinion. \par \par \uc2  A Danish 'No' is expected to weaken Swedish euro support and weaken the crown from its current level, which analysts said was pricing in the view that Sweden would join the euro zone, towards which it directs more than 40 percent of its exports. \par \par \uc2  "There probably is going to be a knock-on effect because of the proximity of the two countries and the economic ties they share," said Juli Collins-Thompson, EMU-outs market economist at BNP Paribas Group in London. \par \par \uc2  "If there is a 'No' vote in Denmark people will think that this can delay Sweden entry. If there is a selling of Swedish assets, there will be some feedthrough effect on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  In March this year Sweden's ruling Social Democrats voted in favour of joining the euro, but a decision will be taken at a referendum that is not expected before autumn 2002. \par \par \uc2  But the latest survey by the Swedish Statistics Office on Wednesday showed that opposition to the euro rose by seven percent to 42, with support falling to 32.6 percent. \par \par \uc2  A member of the European Union since January 1995, Sweden decided not to join the euro at its launch in January 1999. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said memories of damaging exchange-rate fluctuations which surrounded Sweden's decision to abandon its peg to the Ecu, a precursor to the euro, in November 1992 are still engraved in dealers' minds. \par \par \uc2  But as long as the authorities give clear signals of their commitment to monetary and exchange stability, the jitters in the Swedish crown should be limited, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "If the Danish referendum results in a 'No', of which there is a good chance, there is bound to be a pulic opinion effect in Sweden," said Darren Williams, economist at Donaldson, Lufkin and Jenrette in London. \par \par \uc2  "We could see some short-term negative market reaction in Denmark and we could see the same in Sweden, but as long as economic policy (in those countries) continue to be addressed towards stability I do not expect the reaction to go too far." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010820dw6g03huw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
