\par\par \b \uc2  Danish c.bank seen selling euro to support crown. \b0\par\par\uc2 242 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2000\par 16:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  COPENHAGEN, Jan 21 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Friday - selling euros for crowns - dealers said, dubbing the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  undramatic. \par \par \uc2  One \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer said the bank was in the market for the second day running to shore up the crown, buying crowns for some 500 million euros on Friday alone. \par \par \uc2  The bank bought crowns for prices ranging between 7.4455 and 7.4460 crowns per euro, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Last week the bank bought crowns for approximately 700-800 million euros, traders said. \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official declined to comment, citing the bank's stated policy of not discussing its market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in public. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said pressure on the crown did not stem from any speculation against Denmark, but from regular corporate sector interest. \par \par \uc2  Danish investors' needs to convert crowns into foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , amid ongoing portfolio diversification into overseas securities, was also bringing pressure to bear on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The crown - which through most of 1999 traded in a range of 7.4325-7.4375 per euro - stood at 7.4455, slightly lower than late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Since the euro's launch in January 1999, the Danish crown has consistently traded on the stronger side of its 7.46038 ERM-2 central parity. \par \par 