\par\par \b \uc2  Danish c.bank seen selling euro to shore up crown. \b0\par\par\uc2 372 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2000\par 13:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  COPENHAGEN, Jan 12 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Wednesday - selling euros for crowns - dealers said, describing the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as undramatic. \par \par \uc2  One \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer said the bank was in the market for the third day running to shore up the crown while another market maker said small-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been going on for about a week. \par \par \uc2  "We estimate that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has bought Danish crowns for approximately 700 million euros in the past week," Nykredit Markets economist Steen Groendahl told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official declined to comment, referring to the bank's stated policy of not discussing its market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in public. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the stability-oriented \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was buying crowns to cushion a commercially driven weakening of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the intention of preventing excessive intra-day swings versus the euro - its anchor in the ERM-2 grid. \par \par \uc2  The pressure on the crown was not stemming from any speculation against Denmark, but from regular corporate sector commercial interests strengthened by big Danish investors' needs to convert crowns into foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid ongoing portfolio diversification into overseas securities, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  One dealer said short-term investment in the Danish crown had earlier offered a small yield pick-up over the euro zone, ensuring an inflow of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  which tended to largely offset commercial outflows, keeping the crown-euro rate steady. \par \par \uc2  This yield pick-up virtually melted away recently, leaving the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the only market player buying crowns, he said, speaking on condition of anonymity. \par \par \uc2  At 1205 GMT, an overnight investment in the euro yielded 3.02 percent against 3.05 percent for a same-maturity crown investment. \par \par \uc2  The crown - which through most of 1999 traded in a range of 7.4325-7.4375 per euro - stood at 7.4450, just off an intra-day low of 7.4453. \par \par \uc2  Since the euro's launch in January 1999, the Danish crown has consistently traded well on the stronger side of its 7.46038 ERM-2 central parity. \par \par \uc2  ((Copenhagen newsroom +45 33 96 96 50, fax +45 33 93 84 57, copenhagen.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw1c028lh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
