\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Danish rates seen rising more to help DKK. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Peter Starck \par \uc2 722 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 1998\par 14:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  COPENHAGEN, Sept 21 (Reuters) - The Danish crown came under renewed pressure on Monday and analysts said Friday's sharp increase in key interest rates may prove counter-productive, forcing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise rates again to keep the crown steady. \par \par \uc2  For Denmark, the vexing characteristic of the present crown crisis was that it had not been caused by domestic factors. \par \par \uc2  The crown, stable against the German mark for most of the past 15 years, weakened amid a universal investor flight to safe-haven German and U.S. bonds, a trend accentuated in Denmark's case by its status as an EMU non-starter. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the monetary tightening had actually served to alarm some investors who had been unaware of any trouble. \par \par \uc2  "It was definitely a bad move. Denmark implemented a major rate rise on the basis of a minor weakening of the crown," said Sharda Persaud, an economist with Paribas Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "There is not a specific Danish reason for the run on the crown," said Georg Andersen, fixed income strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston (CSFB) in London. \par \par \uc2  "People want German Bunds. We are seeing some adjustments of foreign investors' holdings of Danish bonds across the board," he said. \par \par \uc2  Kai Lindberg, chief economist at Codan Bank, said flows indicated that Danish bonds had been sold for billions of crowns on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The slice of Danish bonds held by overseas investors could fall to around 35 percent from slightly over 40 percent before the turmoil started with the devaluation of the Russian rouble in mid-August, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  A stable crown exchange rate is a cornerstone of Danish economic policy, and analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not hesitate to raise key interest rates more, if necessary, thus making investment in crown-denominated instruments more attractive, and borrowing in crown more expensive. \par \par \uc2  With most household and corporate sector borrowing in long-term mortgage loans, higher short-term market rates would not raise a public outcry or prompt the political establishment to influence the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to moderate its policy. \par \par \uc2  But some analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have shot itself in the foot by Friday's aggressive one percentage point increase to 5.00 percent in two monetary steering tools, the repo and certificate of deposit rate. The tightening came at the end of the fourth consecutive day of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the crown. \par \par \uc2  "It will not take the pressure off the crown," CSFB's Andersen said. "They (the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) can stem the tide but they cannot avert the direction," he said. \par \par \uc2  "There is a perception that we could be in for more rate hikes," he added. \par \par \uc2  "If the pressure continues, and we suppose it will, they will have to raise rates again. The next move will be 200 basis points," said Lindberg at Codan Bank. \par \par \uc2  Persaud at Paribas said the next increase would have to be at least half a percentage point. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Monday it had again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the crown. \par \par \uc2  The lack of a clear-cut result to Sunday's election in neighbouring Sweden also tended to be negative for the Danish crown, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "We are seeing investors leaving the Scandinavian countries. They have left Norway already," Lindberg noted. \par \par \uc2  At 1115 GMT, the crown traded at 3.8129 per mark compared with 3.8118 after Friday's monetary tightening. Monday morning's lows against the mark was 3.8131 before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The yield spread between \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark 7.00 percent 2007 state bond and 4.00 percent 2008 Bunds widened to 111 basis points from Friday's close at 76 basis points. \par \par \uc2  In the two-year maturity, the yield spread shot out to 202 basis points from 116. \par \par \uc2  Denmark's inverted yield curve - two-month rates near 5.50 percent against 4.95 percent for 10-year paper - was regarded as a danger signal by some investors remembering that the same had occurred in Norway, Andersen said. \par \par \uc2  "We expect it (the curve) to stay inverse, even become more inverted next week or so," he said. \par \par \uc2  ((Copenhagen newsroom +45 33 96 96 50, fax +45 33 93 84 57, copenhagen.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9l01isx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
