\par\par \b \uc2  Nordic  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  -2: Danish Krone Slips Despite  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 341 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 1998\par 17:45\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2  the krone (DKK) continued to slip despite a new round of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Danmarks Nationalbank has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  daily to support the shaky krone since last Tuesday, and some analysts estimate it spent at least DKK5 billion doing so last week alone. \par \par \uc2  A director at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed the buying Monday, but declined to specify the magnitude of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or the level at which it took place. \par \par \uc2  Still, by 1510 GMT the Deutsche mark had edged up to DKK3.8128, from DKK 3.8113 in late European trading Friday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday followed on the heels of an aggressive move by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday, when it raised its floor discount and deposit rates by 50 basis points each, to 4.25%, and boosted its repo rate by a full point to 5.00%. \par \par \uc2  The move triggered a massive sell-off of Danish bonds Monday with the negative sentiment spilling over to the krone. \par \par \uc2  In Norway a slightly higher oil price failed to boost the krone (NOK) which fell back late in the session. At 1520 GMT the Deutsche mark fetched NOK4.4460, up from NOK4.4350 late Friday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At 1530 GMT Monday, the following prices were quoted (compared with\par late Friday in Europe):\par                      Deutsche mark      ECU Index\par Swedish SEK         4.7150 vs 4.6315   125.03 vs 122.27\par Danish DKK          3.8128 vs 3.8113    --        --\par Norwegian NOK       4.4460 vs 4.4350   109.13 vs 108.27\par Finnish FIM         3.0450 vs 3.0447     --        --\par TCW Index\par Swedish SEK         126.46 vs 124.14\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The weighted ECU index tracks a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that doesn't\par include the dollar or the yen. A higher index means a strengthening of\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the basket.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Danish krone isn't quoted on a weighted ECU index. Only the\par Swedish krona is quoted on a total, competitive, trade-weighted index.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Anette Jonsson; +46 8 5451 3093\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 21-09-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1545GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1058 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du9l0836f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
