\par\par \b \uc2  WSJE: Slide Continues For  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  In Scandinavia \b0\par\par\uc2 412 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 1998\par 05:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  From Thursday's Wall Street Journal Europe \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    By Almar Latour\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  (Dow Jones)--Scandinavian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued sliding against the U.S. dollar and mark, amid investor worries about the looming collapse of Russia's financial markets and a domino effect of the Asian crisis on emerging markets world-wide. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis also sank its teeth into Scandinavia's political landscape, unleashing more political bickering over fiscal policy in Norway and forcing the issue of European economic and monetary union into Sweden's national election debate. \par \par \uc2  The Norwegian krone, marked by heavy losses against the mark in recent days, again depreciated the most among Scandinavian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , trading at 4.4410 kroner to the mark in late trading, compared with 4.3810 kroner on Tuesday and 4.0900 kroner in early January. The dollar rose to 8.3179 kroner from 8.2655 kroner on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Swedish krona also dropped in value against the mark, although marginally. It traded at 4.6035 kronor against the mark compared with 4.6000 kronor on Tuesday and 4.2688 kronor in late April. The krona, however, had been sliding sharply against the mark all week. The dollar rose to 8.3179 kronor compared with 8.2655 kronor on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Danish krone came under pressure as well and lost in trading against the dollar, as \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unofficial peg to the mark. The Danish krone traded at 6.8840 kroner against the U.S. dollar compared with 6.8590 kroner on Tuesday. The Danish krone remained firm against the mark, at its unofficial peg of about 3.8081 - but only after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the Danish krone against the mark. \par \par \uc2  The depreciation of the Norwegian, Swedish and Danish \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the mark and dollar in recent days shows that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of countries outside the euro zone are more sensitive to external economic crises and investors' caprices than \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that are members of EMU. \par \par \uc2  By contrast, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of EMU members are more solid. For example, Nordic neighbor Finland's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - the markkaa - has proved to be the sole survivor recently in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  carnage. \par \par \uc2  "When there is turbulence, it's hard to stand alone," said Jonas Frycklund, EMU expert for the Federation of Swedish Industries. "That goes for both Sweden and Norway. Other countries simply enjoy more protection." \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 27-08-98 \par \par \uc2  0336GMT \par \par \uc2  1127 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8r07c76\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
