\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CROSS-Mark/Drachma surges on Russia fears. \b0\par\par\uc2 449 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 1998\par 17:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 26 (Reuters) - The mark rose sharply against the drachma on Wednesday despite three \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of Greece as Russia fears took their toll. \par \par \uc2  Having fallen to a three-month low of 174.55, the drachma fought back after the third round of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  support. \par \par \uc2  "Greece is considered the last emerging market in Europe and gets hit when ...things are tough," said Michael Lewis, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Deutsche Morgan Grenfell in London. \par \par \uc2  At 1500 GMT the drachma stood at 173.20/40 compared with 170.60/70 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Norwegian and Swedish crowns extended recent losses against the mark due to Russia and a gloomy outlook for weaker commodity prices, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  But the mark slipped against the yen and dollar after German Finance Minister Theo Waigel said the G7 group of industrialised nations, International Monetary Fund and European Union cannot solve Russia's problems. \par \par \uc2  The mark fell to 80.00/02 yen, down from 80.56/59. \par \par \uc2  Analysts linked the peripheral \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' tumble to a generalised move into core markets in light of Russia's difficulties. \par \par \uc2  "The problems in Russia and emerging market contagion effects are creating their own Scandinavian contagion," said David Brown, chief European economist at Bear Stearns in London. \par \par \uc2  The mark rose 1.9 percent against the Norwegian crown to 4.4595/6.25 against late Tuesday's 4.3770/90. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the crown against the German mark. The Danish crown was at session lows of 3.8080/83 against the mark, compared to Tuesday's late 3.8105/10. \par \par \uc2  "Our stated policy is to keep the crown steady," deputy director Kirsten Mordhorst, told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Sweden followed Norway's lead at first, sinking to 4.6440/55 per mark against 4.5930/60 late on Tuesday. But the Swedish crown staged an afternoon rally to 4.6090/20 on rumours of Riksbank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the pain may continue. \par \par \uc2  "I have no sense that these markets are out of woods yet," Bear Stearns' Brown said. "There will be no risk reversal on mark/Scandinavian crosses until we see recovery in Russia, which is far off." \par \par \uc2  He said the market is unlikely to differentiate between the Nordic \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , despite Norway's higher dependence on oil. \par \par \uc2  But Kjell Tangen, general manager of Christiania Bank in London said he saw little reason for Norway's fall, much less Sweden's follow on. \par \par \uc2  "There is no reason from it apart from that they are small \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  outside the EMU area," he said. ((James Saft, London newsroom + 44 171 542 2734)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8q01key\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
