\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CROSS-Mark/drachma gains despite  \highlight22\uc2 interventions \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 1,062 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 1998\par 14:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Second \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Bank of Greece of Wednesday sees selling of $500 million, 750 million marks and 200 million ecu for drachma. * Drachma falls to levels against the mark not seen since May. Mark/Drachma\tab      session high of 174.00/15 and against late Tuesday's 170.60/70.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    * Mark drops off highs against some after German Finance\par \f28 \par \par \uc2   Minister Waigel says G7, IM and EU cannot solve Russia problems. \par \par \uc2     * Norwegian and Swedish crowns off lows but remain vulnerable. \par \par \uc2  *Mark earlier rallied vs Norway crown to highest since July 1995 as fallout from Russia and commodity weakness hit Nordics. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Mark/Norwegian crown\tab      at 4.4275/05, off high of 4.4450, and against 4.3770/90 in late Europe on Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Spillover effect into Swedish crown drives mark to highest level against Swedish crown since March 1996.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Mark/Swedish crown\tab      at 4.6220/50, off high of 4.6440, and against late Tuesday's 4.5930/60. * Mark falls below 80 yen\tab     , trading at 79.81/9.83, down from 80.56/59.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par MARK REPORT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par LONDON, Aug 26 (Reuters) - The mark wrested new gains against the Norwegian and Swedish crowns on Wednesday on fears of weak commodity prices and Russia's crisis, analysts said. Fallout from the Russian debt restructuring detailed on Tuesday speeded an ongoing generalised flight to quality favouring the mark. "The problems in Russia and emerging market contagion effects are creating their own Scandinavian contagion," said David Brown, chief European economist at Bear Stearns in London.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By 0945 GMT the mark was at 4.4260/90 Norway crowns, just off its 4.4285 peak, the highest since December 1995.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    The mark was up against the Norwegian crown from late\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Tuesday's 4.3770.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Denmark\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it was \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prop up the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  crown against the German mark. The Danish crown was virtually\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  unchanged at 3.8106 against the mark.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Sweden followed Norway's lead, sinking to 4.6280/10 per mark\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  against 4.5930 late on Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    And analysts said the pain may continue.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I have no sense that these markets are out of woods yet," Brown said. "There will be no risk reversal on mark/Scandinavian crosses until we see recovery in Russia, which is far off."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Despite the run for core \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  the Finnish markka has held up better, partly due to Finland's commitment to joining European economic and monetary union, analysts said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    The Finnish markka was slightly lower against the mark at\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  3.0400/30, against late Tuesday's 3.0379/0408.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Bear Stearns' Brown said the market is unlikely to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par differentiate between the Nordic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , despite Norway's higher dependence on oil prices.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But Kjell Tangen, general manager of Christiania Bank London said he saw little reason for Norway's fall, much less Sweden's follow on. "There is no reason from it apart from that they are small \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  outside the EMU area," he said. "I would reckon this is just a temporary situation." The Greek drachma was also pulled into Russia's vortex, falling against the mark and prompting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the Bank of Greece. The drachma was at 171.65/85 against the mark, down from 170.60/70 in late Tuesday trade but above an earlier three-month low at 171.95. Greece's decision on Tuesday to pull the privatisation of Ionian Bank has also dented sentiment towards the drachma. ((James Saft, London newsroom + 44 171 542.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Mark/Swedish crown\tab      at 4.6280/10, off high of 4.6440, and against late Tuesday's 4.5930/60.\par * Drachma dragged into Russia rout and Bank of Greece \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , dragging Drachma off of levels against the mark not seen since May. Mark/Drachma\tab      at 171.72/82, below 172.15 earlier, and against late Tuesday's 170.60/70.\par * Mark falls below 80 yen\tab     , trading at 79.97/80.00, down from 80.56/59.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par MARK REPORT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par LONDON, Aug 26 (Reuters) - The mark wrested new gains against the Norwegian and Swedish crowns on Wednesday on fears of weak commodity prices and Russia's crisis, analysts said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Fallout from the Russian debt restructuring detailed on Tuesday speeded an ongoing generalised flight to quality favouring the mark.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The problems in Russia and emerging market contagion effects are creating their own Scandinavian contagion," said David Brown, chief European economist at Bear Stearns in London.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By 0945 GMT the mark was at 4.4260/90 Norway crowns, just off its 4.4285 peak, the highest since December 1995.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The mark was up against the Norwegian crown from late Tuesday's 4.3770.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Denmark\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it was \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prop up the crown against the German mark. The Danish crown was virtually unchanged at 3.8106 against the mark.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Sweden followed Norway's lead, sinking to 4.6280/10 per mark against 4.5930 late on Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And analysts said the pain may continue.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I have no sense that these markets are out of woods yet," Brown said. "There will be no risk reversal on mark/Scandinavian crosses until we see recovery in Russia, which is far off."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Despite the run for core \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  the Finnish markka has held up better, partly due to Finland's commitment to joining European economic and monetary union, analysts said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Finnish markka was slightly lower against the mark at 3.0400/30, against late Tuesday's 3.0379/0408.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bear Stearns' Brown said the market is unlikely to differentiate between the Nordic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , despite Norway's higher dependence on oil prices.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But Kjell Tangen, general manager of Christiania Bank London said he saw little reason for Norway's fall, much less Sweden's follow on.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There is no reason from it apart from that they are small \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  outside the EMU area," he said. "I would reckon this is just a temporary situation."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Greek drachma was also pulled into Russia's vortex, falling against the mark and prompting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the Bank of Greece.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The drachma was at 171.65/85 against the mark, down from 170.60/70 in late Tuesday trade but above an earlier three-month low at 171.95.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Greece's decision on Tuesday to pull the privatisation of Ionian Bank has also dented sentiment towards the drachma.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((James Saft, London newsroom + 44 171 542 6284)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8q01jw9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
