\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  CROSS - Mark up on Nordic contagion. \b0\par\par\uc2 411 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 1998\par 12:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 26 (Reuters) - The mark wrested new gains against the Norwegian and Swedish crowns on Wednesday on fears of weak commodity prices and Russia's crisis, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Fallout from the Russian debt restructuring detailed on Tuesday speeded an ongoing generalised flight to quality favouring the mark. \par \par \uc2  "The problems in Russia and emerging market contagion effects are creating their own Scandinavian contagion," said David Brown, chief European economist at Bear Stearns in London. \par \par \uc2  By 0945 GMT the mark was at 4.4260/90 Norway crowns, just off its 4.4285 peak, the highest since December 1995. \par \par \uc2  The mark was up against the Norwegian crown from late Tuesday's 4.3770. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Denmark\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the crown against the German mark. The Danish crown was virtually unchanged at 3.8106 against the mark. \par \par \uc2  Sweden followed Norway's lead, sinking to 4.6280/10 per mark against 4.5930 late on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  And analysts said the pain may continue. \par \par \uc2  "I have no sense that these markets are out of woods yet," Brown said. "There will be no risk reversal on mark/Scandinavian crosses until we see recovery in Russia, which is far off." \par \par \uc2  Despite the run for core \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  the Finnish markka has held up better, partly due to Finland's commitment to joining European economic and monetary union, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The Finnish markka was slightly lower against the mark at 3.0400/30, against late Tuesday's 3.0379/0408. \par \par \uc2  Bear Stearns' Brown said the market is unlikely to differentiate between the Nordic \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , despite Norway's higher dependence on oil prices. \par \par \uc2  But Kjell Tangen, general manager of Christiania Bank London said he saw little reason for Norway's fall, much less Sweden's follow on. \par \par \uc2  "There is no reason from it apart from that they are small \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  outside the EMU area," he said. "I would reckon this is just a temporary situation." \par \par \uc2  The Greek drachma was also pulled into Russia's vortex, falling against the mark and prompting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of Greece. \par \par \uc2  The drachma was at 171.65/85 against the mark, down from 170.60/70 in late Tuesday trade but above an earlier three-month low at 171.95. \par \par \uc2  Greece's decision on Tuesday to pull the privatisation of Ionian Bank has also dented sentiment towards the drachma. \par \par \uc2  ((James Saft, London newsroom + 44 171 542 6284)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8q01ln0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
